## Finding NODE-R1 — P2: normal reconciliation cannot upgrade a published v1 checkpoint

Primary changed-code location: `src/services/codexRolloutCollector/index.js:626–628`. Call chain: `src/services/codexHistoryReconciliationScheduler.js:167–191` and `analyzerProcessManager.js:615–619`.

The scheduler selects `append` whenever product SQLite already has a canonical checkpoint and nonempty cursor. The upgraded manager's default hello negotiates `history_semantics_v2` and selects `target-v2`. The collector clears the v1 anchor/cursor only when the caller already selected `backfill`; the scheduler never selects that route for an existing canonical record. Rust therefore correctly rejects the ordinary append as `checkpoint_incompatible`. The scheduler retains the intent and old canonical, but subsequent terminal observations keep taking the same failing route, so new completed content cannot be saved.

This is an actual retained-state compatibility case: the original persistent product DB still contains conversation F `01a0ee04-a775-72b3-bd07-04aac804a3b4` with canonical `target-v1` checkpoint and nonempty cursor (checkpoint SHA256 `fc91b4024979e4425e4233fc5946b72c5dd12470c1db6512a0e9b3576f3ced87`). No live operation was performed on F.

A fresh isolated real-process reproduction durably published a genuine v1 prefix into SQLite through Node importer, positive ACK and Rust complete. After adding a genuine fixture completion row, the actual scheduler sent v2 hello followed by append with v1 anchor and cursor. Observed error: `checkpoint_incompatible: persisted reducer profile is incompatible with negotiated read semantics`, retryable=false. Product items remained 2→2, the completion was absent, and both old canonical and product checkpoint hashes remained unchanged. The harness returned exit0 because it captured this failure; it is not a product PASS. Exact safe stdout and setup are in `node-audit.json#/new_reproducer`.

Affected delivery: 832 S4/S5 accepting the 833 Node profile/request bridge. S4 execution `key-8ddef1358f673636db8e4923d31b087c504694e05340b8cb94dd2e0757671df0`; S5 execution `key-d0b8dc2e154ae495d2861d6b345314a0f56fc0c50b8709819f3208d1620f4517`. This does not invalidate the scoped S2 design, Rust's correct strict rejection, or the specified v2 N/C/A S6 samples.

Minimal follow-up: select a distinct ordinary backfill job with the four old recovery fields empty when a settled v1 product checkpoint meets negotiated v2. Preserve the original checkpoint/receipts, unsettled original profile/job/delivery retry, and strict cross-profile append rejection. Add a regression through the actual production scheduler and a durably published v1 product checkpoint. The passing direct-collector new-backfill test bypasses this missing selection.

